.\" Corrected for the BESM-6 port, task C7.  Read with `nroff -man'.
.TH TAR 1
.SH NAME
tar  \-  archiver
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tar
key
.B f
archive
[
.B b
factor ]
[ name ... ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Tar
saves and restores files.
Its actions are controlled by the
.I key
argument.
The
.I key
is a string of characters containing
at most one function letter and possibly
one or more function modifiers.
Other arguments to the command are file or directory
names specifying which files are to be dumped or restored.
In all cases, appearance of a directory name refers to
the files and (recursively) subdirectories of that directory.
.PP
.B "Note:"
v7's
.I tar
wrote on magnetic tape and defaulted to
.BR /dev/mt1 .
This machine has no tape drive and no driver for one, so the
.B f
modifier is
.BR mandatory :
an archive is a file, a pipe, or a raw disk device.
The drive-selecting digits
.B 0
through
.B 7
are gone with the default, and so is the
.B u
key; see NOTES.
.PP
The function portion of
the key is specified by one of the following letters:
.TP 8
.B  r
The named files
are written
on the end of the archive.
The
.B c
function implies this.
.TP 8
.B  x
The named files are extracted from the archive.
If the named file matches a directory whose contents
had been written onto the archive, this directory is (recursively) extracted.
The owner, modification time, and mode are restored (if possible).
If no file argument is given, the entire content of the
archive is extracted.
Note that if multiple entries specifying the same file
are on the archive, the last one overwrites
all earlier.
.TP 8
.B  t
The names of the specified files are listed each time they occur
on the archive.
If no file argument is given,
all of the names on the archive are listed.
.TP 8
.B  c
Create a new archive; writing begins at the beginning
of the archive instead of after the last file.
This command implies
.B r.
.PP
The following characters may be used in addition to the letter
which selects the function desired.
.TP 10
.B  v
Normally
.I tar
does its work silently.
The
.B v
(verbose)
option causes it to type the name of each file it treats
preceded by the function letter.
With the
.B t
function,
.B v
gives more information about the
archive entries than just the name.
.TP 10
.B  w
causes
.I tar
to print the action to be taken followed by file name, then
wait for user confirmation. If a word beginning with `y'
is given, the action is performed. Any other input, and
end of file, means
don't do it.
.TP 10
.B f
causes
.I tar
to use the next argument as the name of the archive.
It is not optional.
If the name of the file is `\-', tar writes to
standard output or reads from standard input, whichever is
appropriate. Thus,
.I tar
can be used as the head or tail of a filter chain
.I Tar
can also be used to move hierarchies with the command
.ce 1
cd fromdir; tar cf \- . | (cd todir; tar xf \-)
.TP 10
.B b
causes
.I tar
to use the next argument as the blocking factor for
records. The default is 1 for a file or a pipe and 6 for a raw
device; the maximum is 20.
Reading needs no blocking factor at all and ignores this one:
records come out of the buffer in the order they went in
however they were grouped.
See NOTES for the rule a raw device imposes.
.TP 10
.B l
tells
.I tar
to complain if it cannot resolve all of the links
to the files dumped. If this is not specified, no
error messages are printed.
.TP 10
.B m
tells
.I tar
to not restore the modification times.
The mod time
will be the time of extraction.
.SH NOTES
.SS "A NAME IS 100 BYTES OUT AND 18 BACK"
The archive format gives a name 100 bytes, and a name of exactly 100
carries no terminator.
A directory entry on this filesystem holds
.B 18
.RB ( DIRSIZ ,
see
.IR types (5)),
so a path with long components is archived faithfully and
.B cannot be restored under the same name:
each component is truncated to 18 characters as it is created.
Names survive the round trip in one direction only.
A path longer than 100 bytes is refused outright, with a diagnostic,
and so is a directory whose own path leaves no room for the names under it.
.SS "AN ARCHIVE ON A RAW DEVICE"
.I Tar
can write straight onto
.B /dev/rmd1
without a filesystem on it, which is how a tree leaves this machine.
A raw transfer goes through
.IR physio (),
which requires the count to be a whole number of 3072-byte filesystem blocks
and the buffer to lie on a 512-word boundary.
512 times the blocking factor is a multiple of 3072 only when the factor
is a multiple of
.BR 6 ,
so on a character special the default is 6 and a factor that is not a
multiple of 6 is refused with a diagnostic.
.PP
.B r
is refused on a raw device.
Appending means stepping back over the archive's terminating record, and a
seek of one 512-byte record is not a whole number of blocks;
.IR physio ()
truncates such an offset silently and would position the write on the wrong
block.
.PP
Nothing stops
.I tar
being pointed at
.BR /dev/rmd0 ,
which is the pack the system is running on.
It is mode 0600 and root's for that reason.
.SS "WHAT WAS REMOVED"
The
.B u
key is not implemented.
It kept an index of names and modification times in
.B /tmp
and sorted and de-duplicated it by running
.IR sort (1)
and
.IR awk (1)
through
.IR sh (1);
there is no
.I awk
on this system.
Use
.B c
to rebuild an archive or
.B r
to add to one.
.PP
The
.B 0
through
.B 7
modifiers, which selected a tape drive, are not implemented.
.SS "BLOCKS HERE ARE 512 BYTES"
An archive record is 512 bytes and a physical block is 512 times the
blocking factor.
These are the
.I archive's
units and not this filesystem's, whose block is 3072 bytes: the numbers
are the format's, they are the same on every machine that has ever written
a tar archive, and changing them would make an archive written here
readable nowhere else.
.I Tar
is the exception to the rule that a block reported to a user is 1024 bytes
.RI ( df (1M)).
.SH FILES
/bin/mkdir
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Complaints about bad key characters and archive read/write errors.
.br
Complaints if enough memory is not available to hold
the link tables.
.SH BUGS
There is no way to ask for the
.IR n -th
occurrence of a file.
.br
Device errors are handled ungracefully.
.br
The
.B b
option should not be used with archives that are
going to be updated.
.br
A name is stored exactly as it was given on the command line, so
.B "tar cf arch /a/b"
stores
.B /a/b/...
and extracting it later writes over the original wherever the extraction
is done from.
Name the tree relatively \- the
.B f
entry's
.B "cd fromdir"
idiom \- unless that is what is wanted.
.br
No record is written for a directory itself, only for the files under it,
so a directory that is empty is not archived and the modes of the
directories an extraction creates are
.IR mkdir (1)'s
and not the original's.
.br
The current limit on file name length is
100 characters, and a restored name is limited to 18 per component;
see NOTES.
.br
A file that cannot be opened, or whose name is too long, is reported and
skipped, and the exit status is still zero.
.br
The walk is limited to 12 levels of directory nesting: it holds one open
descriptor per level, and
.B NOFILE
is 20.
